Exceptions
- a runtime error not caught by compiler (e.g ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException); an object in Java
- terminates program execution if exception is not handled(terminating program: bad, letting program execution to continue: good)
- exceptions can be countered with java mechanics and allows the program to execute completely

Exception handling
- three operations:
	- declaring an exception / public void readFile() throws IOException { . . . }
	- throwing an exception / throws new IllegalArgumentException("Number must be above zero.");
	- catching an exception / try-catch example
- before exceptions, error checking was handled with if statements
- using exceptions will enable a method to throw an exception to its caller and let them handle it however they would like
- Without it, a method must handle the exception or terminate a program
- Program termination is very unacceptable

try-catch block
- surrounds code that throws an exception
- try keyword watches for exception
- catch keyword handles the exception
- throwing an exception - happens when throw is explicitly called, or exception happens in try/catch block
- the benefits are:
	-the caller handles the exception and not the author
	- Client decided how to handle an exception
	- Separates detecting error from handling error
	- flexibility

Exception types
- root class is java.lang.Throwable
- exceptions can be created by extending the Exception class or subclasses of Exception
- system errors, exception, runtime exceptions are the three major exception types(ofc there's more than one yipeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeee)

System errors
- thrown by JVM and represented in the Error class
- numerous examples include LinkageError, VirtualMachineError and more

Exception errors
- errors caused by your program and external circumstances
- numerous examples include ClassNotFoundException, IOException, RuntimeException and more

Runtime exceptions
- **note the RuntimeException class extends Exception**
- caused by programming errors
- numerous examples include ArithmeticException, NullPointerException, IndexOutOfBoundsException, IllegalArgumentException and more

Checked v. unchecked exceptions
- RuntimeException, Error and their subclasses are considered unchecked exceptions
- All else is checked
- checked exceptions should be surrounded by try-catch blocks or declared in method header so caller must deal with it
- unchecked exceptions reflect programming logic errors that are unrecoverable

Throwing exceptions
- creating an instance of appropriate exception type and throw
- throws declares exceptions
- throw throws

Catching exceptions
- use try-catch block to surround code that can throw exceptions
- inside the try block:
	- if there is **no** exception error, the catch block is skipped
	- if there is an exception error, the rest try block is skipped and the execution is passed to the catch block
- Exception Handler - code that handles exception, found by propagating the exception through all calling methods until it is caught
- can catch subclasses of exceptions and handle differently
- superclasses will catch a subclass first
- methods that declare that they throw exceptions should be called in try-catch blocks

Declaring exceptions
- every method must state types of checked exceptions it might explicitly throw its header

Catch or declare checked expressions
- if a method declares a checked exception, you must either:
	- invoke a try-catch block 
	- OR declare the exception in method header to indicate possible exception in calling method
	
java.lang.Throwable methods
- getMessage("can be set on creation here in constructor like this") - returns message for the Exception.
- toString() - textual representation of Exception object, does not include trace: Classname: message
- printStackTrace() - prints stack trace
- getStackTrace() - returns a StackTraceElement

finally keyword
- executed regardless of exception; added after catch 
- if there are no exceptions, the finally body is executed

Custom Exceptions
- Creating custom exceptions are possible, but using exception classes in API are recommended, unless predefined classes are not sufficient

